// The host this package ships: the guard that decides every effect a program
// performs, written in EYG and run by the plain installed `eyg` binary. Nothing
// in the CLI knows about this file. The tool writes a per-call entry that imports
// it, the policy and the program, and calls `build`:
//
//   let guard = import "<this file>"
//   let policy = import "<policy module>"
//   let ctx = {roots: [...], cwd: "...", fields: [...], protected: [...]}
//   guard.build(policy.rules(ctx), ctx, (_) -> {
//     let program = import "<program file written for this call>"
//     program.run({})
//   })
//
// `roots`, `cwd` and `fields` are values a policy reads. `protected` is read by
// the guard itself, and a hand-written entry has to write it too: a call whose
// ctx omits it gets a missing-field error at its first write effect rather than
// an unguarded write.
//
// # Why a handler per effect
//
// `handle Name(handler, body)` runs `body` with `handler` installed: an effect
// the program performs inside it reaches the handler, and the host performs
// nothing for that label. So the guard names every effect the CLI offers - one
// clause per row of `guides/eyg-surface.tsv` - and anything it does not name is
// outside the policy: a label the CLI's own host implements is performed by that
// host (the four unchecked effects, and its internal `Abort`, which a program can
// perform and which only stops the program), and any other label has no host and
// answers `unhandled effect`. Naming every surface row is what makes the default
// a refusal: there is no label with reach that the guard leaves unnamed.
//
// # The contract a policy rule keeps
//
// A rule is a function of the value the program asked for. It answers
//
//   Pass(value)   the guard performs the effect for real, with the value the
//                 rule handed back, and answers the program with the host's own
//                 reply. The rule may rewrite that value; it may not skip it.
//   Mock(reason)  the guard performs nothing and answers the program itself.
//                 `reason` is the text the program sees, and it is what makes a
//                 denial readable.
//
// There is no `Pass` in the language: inside a handler the way to perform is to
// `perform` again, which the handler's own clause does not catch, and the way to
// refuse is to answer with a value yourself. Each effect answers a refusal in the
// shape its own reply type allows:
//
//   ReadFile, WriteFile, AppendFile, DeleteFile, MakeDirectory, ReadDirectory,
//   Search, Fetch, StandardIn, CreateKey
//                        Error(reason)
//   Env                  None({})  - an unset variable is the honest answer
//   CWD                  Error({}) - the reply's Error carries no text on this host
//   Sleep, StandardOut, StandardError
//                        the unit value: the effect did not happen
//   Now, Random, Flip    the program stops with `Aborted with reason: <reason>`.
//                        A reply of Integer or True|False cannot carry a refusal,
//                        and inventing a number would be a lie, so the reason ends
//                        the program instead.
//   Exit                  Pass(code) ends the program with that status; a refusal
//                        cannot be answered (Exit has no reply) so it aborts too.
//
// # Paths
//
// A path reaches a handler exactly as the program wrote it, which may be relative
// to a working directory the roots do not include. The guard turns it into an
// absolute path with no `.` or `..` segment before the rule sees it, and performs
// the effect with that path, so a rule compares whole paths and a refusal names
// one the program can act on.
//
// # Search
//
// The installed binary has no `Search` effect - the label exists in the language
// and the CLI does not perform it - so the guard is the host for it, and a policy
// decides it in its own `search` field. (The CLI folded `Search` into the
// `read_file` field; one field per effect here is what the checker accepts, and a
// policy that wants the old pairing writes the same rule into both fields.) Then
// the guard walks the tree itself with `ReadDirectory`, which is a guarded effect,
// so every directory it lists is decided by the `read_directory` rule before the
// host reads it. Matching
// is over the path relative to the searched directory: `*` in a segment matches
// any run of characters in that segment, `**` as a whole segment matches any
// number of segments, anything else compares equal, and at most one `*` per
// segment is honoured. Returned paths are absolute, up to `limit` of them.
//
// # What the guard does not do
//
// It never reads the program's text: the model's code is imported inside the
// guarded body, in its own file, so nothing it writes can reach this module. The
// four effects the CLI marks unchecked (DecodeJSON, EYGParse, Hash, Sign) are
// recomputation over values the program already holds, so no rule decides them and
// the guard re-performs them. `Search` is a walk, not a host effect, so a search
// costs one decision for the root and one per directory it lists.
//
// # Self-protection
//
// The entry imports the guard and the policy by absolute path, and the roots a
// session gets are its working directory, so a session run inside this package
// has the files that decide it inside its own roots. A program that rewrote
// `host/guard.eyg` or a policy module would be writing the rules for its next
// call, which is the whole decision for that call. So the guard refuses
// `write_file`, `append_file`, `delete_file` and `make_directory` for any path
// under `ctx.protected` before the policy rule is consulted, and the tool writes
// that list as the package's own `host/` and `policies/` plus the policy module
// resolved for this call wherever it lives. It is the guard that refuses, not a
// rule, so no policy - including one of the operator's own, or one a session
// asked to be wider - opens those paths, and a wider root does not either.
//
// Reads are not covered: reading the guard and the policies is how a program
// learns what it may ask for, and a read changes nothing. A program that needs
// different rules asks the operator, who changes the policy outside a call.
//
// # The refusal record
//
// A refusal is a value the program may ignore: `let _ = perform ReadFile(...)`
// prints nothing and the call still exits 0. So the guard keeps the refusals it
// made as its own value - `build` returns a `List(String)` of record lines, one per
// refusal, in the order they happened, and the entry writes them to a file of the
// call as the last thing it does. Each line is `<label>|<reason>`, split at the first
// `|`, so a reason may hold `|`. A refusal that the program never printed still has a
// line, and the tool turns the ones it cannot find in the program's own output into
// rows in the call's result.
//
// Two bounds on that record. An aborted run has none: a refusal of `now`, `random`,
// `flip` or `exit`, or the program's own `Abort`, stops the program before the entry
// gets to its last line, so only the CLI's own `Aborted with reason:` survives. And
// the record file is in the call's scratch directory, outside every root, written by
// the entry after the guarded body, so no policy decides that write and no policy can
// refuse it - but a program cannot read or overwrite it either, and it gains no reach
// from its presence.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------- strings, paths

let append = (a, b) -> {
  !string_append(a, b)
}

// Split on `/` into a flat list; `!string_split` returns one head and a tail.
let parts = (text) -> {
  let {head, tail} = !string_split(text, "/")
  [head, ..tail]
}

let reverse = (items) -> {
  !list_fold(items, [], (item, acc) -> {
    [item, ..acc]
  })
}

let join_parts = (segments) -> {
  !list_fold(segments, "", (segment, acc) -> {
    match !equal(acc, "") {
      True(_) -> { segment }
      False(_) -> { append(acc, append("/", segment)) }
    }
  })
}

// Drop empty and `.` segments and apply `..` lexically. The stack is reversed
// while walking, so `..` removes the last real segment.
let collapse = (segments) -> {
  reverse(!list_fold(segments, [], (segment, stack) -> {
    match !equal(segment, "") {
      True(_) -> { stack }
      False(_) -> {
        match !equal(segment, ".") {
          True(_) -> { stack }
          False(_) -> {
            match !equal(segment, "..") {
              True(_) -> {
                match !list_pop(stack) {
                  Error(_) -> { [] }
                  Ok({tail}) -> { tail }
                }
              }
              False(_) -> { [segment, ..stack] }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }))
}

// The absolute, lexically normalized form of a path written by the program.
let absolute = (cwd, path) -> {
  let raw = match !string_starts_with(path, "/") {
    True(_) -> { path }
    False(_) -> { append(cwd, append("/", path)) }
  }
  append("/", join_parts(collapse(parts(raw))))
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------ self-protection

// Equal to `root` or inside it, compared with a trailing `/` so `/a/policies-x`
// is not under `/a/policies`. Paths arrive absolute and collapsed.
let is_under = (root, path) -> {
  let root = match !string_ends_with(root, "/") {
    True(_) -> { root }
    False(_) -> { append(root, "/") }
  }
  match !equal(append(path, "/"), root) {
    True(_) -> { True({}) }
    False(_) -> { !string_starts_with(path, root) }
  }
}

let under_any = (roots, path) -> {
  !list_fold(roots, False({}), (root, found) -> {
    match found {
      True(_) -> { True({}) }
      False(_) -> { is_under(root, path) }
    }
  })
}

// A change to a file the host decides with is refused whatever the rule says, and
// the reason says `denied by the host` so a program can tell this refusal from one
// a policy made and knows asking for a grant will not open the path.
let shield = (effect, ctx, path, decision) -> {
  match under_any(ctx.protected, path) {
    True(_) -> {
      Mock(append(effect, append(" denied by the host: ", append(path, " decides what this call may do"))))
    }
    False(_) -> {
      decision
    }
  }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------ the Search walk

// One segment of a glob against one path segment. No `*` compares equal; one `*`
// fixes the prefix and the suffix around it.
let match_segment = (pattern, name) -> {
  match !string_split_once(pattern, "*") {
    Error(_) -> { !equal(pattern, name) }
    Ok({pre, post}) -> {
      let prefix_ok = match !equal(pre, "") {
        True(_) -> { True({}) }
        False(_) -> { !string_starts_with(name, pre) }
      }
      match prefix_ok {
        True(_) -> {
          match !equal(post, "") {
            True(_) -> { True({}) }
            False(_) -> { !string_ends_with(name, post) }
          }
        }
        False(_) -> { False({}) }
      }
    }
  }
}

// Pattern segments against path segments, where `**` swallows any number of them.
// `!fix` builds the self-recursive function; the argument is one record because
// a fixed function takes one value.
let match_segments = !fix((self) -> {
  (state) -> {
    match !list_pop(state.patterns) {
      Error(_) -> {
        match !list_pop(state.names) {
          Error(_) -> { True({}) }
          Ok(_) -> { False({}) }
        }
      }
      Ok({head, tail}) -> {
        match !equal(head, "**") {
          True(_) -> {
            match self({patterns: tail, names: state.names}) {
              True(_) -> { True({}) }
              False(_) -> {
                match !list_pop(state.names) {
                  Error(_) -> { False({}) }
                  Ok({tail: rest}) -> {
                    self({patterns: state.patterns, names: rest})
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          }
          False(_) -> {
            match !list_pop(state.names) {
              Error(_) -> { False({}) }
              Ok({head: name, tail: rest}) -> {
                match match_segment(head, name) {
                  True(_) -> { self({patterns: tail, names: rest}) }
                  False(_) -> { False({}) }
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
})

let glob_matches = (pattern, path) -> {
  match_segments({patterns: parts(pattern), names: parts(path)})
}

// One entry of one listed directory into the walk's state: `found` is reversed,
// `queue` holds directories still to list, `count` is how many were reported.
let walk_entry = (from, entry, pattern, limit, acc) -> {
  let child = append(from.dir, append("/", entry.name))
  let rel = match !equal(from.rel, "") {
    True(_) -> { entry.name }
    False(_) -> { append(from.rel, append("/", entry.name)) }
  }
  let matched = glob_matches(pattern, rel)
  let with_match = match matched {
    True(_) -> {
      match !int_compare(acc.count, limit) {
        Lt({}) -> { {found: [child, ..acc.found], queue: acc.queue, count: !int_add(acc.count, 1)} }
        Eq({}) -> { acc }
        Gt({}) -> { acc }
      }
    }
    False(_) -> { acc }
  }
  match entry.type {
    Directory(_) -> { {found: with_match.found, queue: [{dir: child, rel: rel}, ..with_match.queue], count: with_match.count} }
    File(_) -> { with_match }
  }
}

// Breadth-first over the directory tree, stopping at `limit` paths. Each
// `ReadDirectory` is performed for real, so the `read_directory` rule decides it
// first; a directory it refuses is skipped, never listed.
let walk = (root, pattern, limit) -> {
  let step = !fix((self) -> {
    (state) -> {
      match !int_compare(state.count, limit) {
        Lt({}) -> {
          match !list_pop(state.queue) {
            Error(_) -> { state }
            Ok({head, tail}) -> {
              let listing = perform ReadDirectory(head.dir)
              match listing {
                Error(_) -> { self({found: state.found, queue: tail, count: state.count}) }
                Ok(entries) -> {
                  self(!list_fold(entries, {found: state.found, queue: tail, count: state.count}, (entry, acc) -> {
                    walk_entry(head, entry, pattern, limit, acc)
                  }))
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }
        Eq({}) -> { state }
        Gt({}) -> { state }
      }
    }
  })
  reverse(step({found: [], queue: [{dir: root, rel: ""}], count: 0}).found)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the handlers
//
// Each handler reads its field out of the policy record, then either performs the
// effect for real or answers the refusal. `read_directory` sits outside `search`
// in `build` below, because the search walk performs `ReadDirectory` and must be
// inside the rule that decides it.

let with_read_file = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle ReadFile((request, resume) -> {
    let path = absolute(ctx.cwd, request.path)
    let decision = policy.read_file({path: path, offset: request.offset, limit: request.limit})
    match decision {
      Pass(request2) -> {
        let reply = perform ReadFile({path: request2.path, offset: request2.offset, limit: request2.limit})
        resume(reply)
      }
      Mock(reason) -> {
        let rest = resume(Error(reason))
        [!string_append("read_file|", reason), ..rest]
      }
    }
  }, cont)
}

let with_read_directory = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle ReadDirectory((path, resume) -> {
    let decision = policy.read_directory(absolute(ctx.cwd, path))
    match decision {
      Pass(path2) -> {
        let reply = perform ReadDirectory(path2)
        resume(reply)
      }
      Mock(reason) -> {
        let rest = resume(Error(reason))
        [!string_append("read_directory|", reason), ..rest]
      }
    }
  }, cont)
}

let with_search = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle Search((request, resume) -> {
    let path = absolute(ctx.cwd, request.path)
    let decision = policy.search({path: path, pattern: request.pattern, limit: request.limit})
    match decision {
      Pass(request2) -> {
        let found = walk(request2.path, request2.pattern, request2.limit)
        resume(Ok(found))
      }
      Mock(reason) -> {
        let rest = resume(Error(reason))
        [!string_append("search|", reason), ..rest]
      }
    }
  }, cont)
}

let with_write_file = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle WriteFile((request, resume) -> {
    let path = absolute(ctx.cwd, request.path)
    let decision = shield("write_file", ctx, path, policy.write_file({path: path, contents: request.contents}))
    match decision {
      Pass(request2) -> {
        let reply = perform WriteFile({path: request2.path, contents: request2.contents})
        resume(reply)
      }
      Mock(reason) -> {
        let rest = resume(Error(reason))
        [!string_append("write_file|", reason), ..rest]
      }
    }
  }, cont)
}

let with_append_file = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle AppendFile((request, resume) -> {
    let path = absolute(ctx.cwd, request.path)
    let decision = shield("append_file", ctx, path, policy.append_file({path: path, contents: request.contents}))
    match decision {
      Pass(request2) -> {
        let reply = perform AppendFile({path: request2.path, contents: request2.contents})
        resume(reply)
      }
      Mock(reason) -> {
        let rest = resume(Error(reason))
        [!string_append("append_file|", reason), ..rest]
      }
    }
  }, cont)
}

let with_delete_file = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle DeleteFile((asked, resume) -> {
    let path = absolute(ctx.cwd, asked)
    let decision = shield("delete_file", ctx, path, policy.delete_file(path))
    match decision {
      Pass(path2) -> {
        let reply = perform DeleteFile(path2)
        resume(reply)
      }
      Mock(reason) -> {
        let rest = resume(Error(reason))
        [!string_append("delete_file|", reason), ..rest]
      }
    }
  }, cont)
}

let with_make_directory = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle MakeDirectory((asked, resume) -> {
    let path = absolute(ctx.cwd, asked)
    let decision = shield("make_directory", ctx, path, policy.make_directory(path))
    match decision {
      Pass(path2) -> {
        let reply = perform MakeDirectory(path2)
        resume(reply)
      }
      Mock(reason) -> {
        let rest = resume(Error(reason))
        [!string_append("make_directory|", reason), ..rest]
      }
    }
  }, cont)
}

let with_cwd = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle CWD((request, resume) -> {
    let decision = policy.cwd(request)
    match decision {
      Pass(value) -> {
        let reply = perform CWD(value)
        resume(reply)
      }
      Mock(reason) -> {
        let rest = resume(Error({}))
        [!string_append("cwd|", reason), ..rest]
      }
    }
  }, cont)
}

let with_env = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle Env((name, resume) -> {
    let decision = policy.env(name)
    match decision {
      Pass(name2) -> {
        let reply = perform Env(name2)
        resume(reply)
      }
      Mock(reason) -> {
        let rest = resume(None({}))
        [!string_append("env|", reason), ..rest]
      }
    }
  }, cont)
}

let with_fetch = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle Fetch((request, resume) -> {
    let decision = policy.fetch(request)
    match decision {
      Pass(request2) -> {
        let reply = perform Fetch(request2)
        resume(reply)
      }
      Mock(reason) -> {
        let rest = resume(Error(reason))
        [!string_append("fetch|", reason), ..rest]
      }
    }
  }, cont)
}

let with_standard_in = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle StandardIn((request, resume) -> {
    let decision = policy.standard_in(request)
    match decision {
      Pass(value) -> {
        let reply = perform StandardIn(value)
        resume(reply)
      }
      Mock(reason) -> {
        let rest = resume(Error(reason))
        [!string_append("standard_in|", reason), ..rest]
      }
    }
  }, cont)
}

let with_create_key = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle CreateKey((request, resume) -> {
    let decision = policy.create_key(request)
    match decision {
      Pass(value) -> {
        let reply = perform CreateKey(value)
        resume(reply)
      }
      Mock(reason) -> {
        let rest = resume(Error(reason))
        [!string_append("create_key|", reason), ..rest]
      }
    }
  }, cont)
}

// A reply of Integer or True|False cannot carry a refusal, so the reason ends the
// program instead of being replaced by an invented value.
let with_now = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle Now((request, resume) -> {
    let decision = policy.now(request)
    match decision {
      Pass(value) -> {
        let reply = perform Now(value)
        resume(reply)
      }
      // An abort ends the program here, so the entry never reaches its last line and
      // this refusal gets no record line - the CLI's own `Aborted with reason:` is all
      // that survives of it.
      Mock(reason) -> { resume(!never(perform Abort(reason))) }
    }
  }, cont)
}

let with_random = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle Random((request, resume) -> {
    let decision = policy.random(request)
    match decision {
      Pass(value) -> {
        let reply = perform Random(value)
        resume(reply)
      }
      Mock(reason) -> { resume(!never(perform Abort(reason))) }
    }
  }, cont)
}

let with_flip = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle Flip((request, resume) -> {
    let decision = policy.flip(request)
    match decision {
      Pass(value) -> {
        let reply = perform Flip(value)
        resume(reply)
      }
      Mock(reason) -> { resume(!never(perform Abort(reason))) }
    }
  }, cont)
}

// The unit reply is the honest answer for an effect that changes nothing the
// program can see: the effect did not happen.
let with_sleep = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle Sleep((request, resume) -> {
    let decision = policy.sleep(request)
    match decision {
      Pass(value) -> {
        let reply = perform Sleep(value)
        resume(reply)
      }
      Mock(reason) -> {
        let rest = resume({})
        [!string_append("sleep|", reason), ..rest]
      }
    }
  }, cont)
}

let with_standard_out = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle StandardOut((text, resume) -> {
    let decision = policy.standard_out(text)
    match decision {
      Pass(text2) -> {
        let reply = perform StandardOut(text2)
        resume(reply)
      }
      Mock(reason) -> {
        let rest = resume({})
        [!string_append("standard_out|", reason), ..rest]
      }
    }
  }, cont)
}

let with_standard_error = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle StandardError((text, resume) -> {
    let decision = policy.standard_error(text)
    match decision {
      Pass(text2) -> {
        let reply = perform StandardError(text2)
        resume(reply)
      }
      Mock(reason) -> {
        let rest = resume({})
        [!string_append("standard_error|", reason), ..rest]
      }
    }
  }, cont)
}

// Exit ends the program; a rule may rewrite the status with Pass, and a refusal
// cannot be answered because Exit has no reply, so it aborts with the reason.
let with_exit = (policy, ctx, cont) -> {
  handle Exit((status, resume) -> {
    let decision = policy.exit(status)
    match decision {
      Pass(code) -> { !never(perform Exit(code)) }
      Mock(reason) -> { !never(perform Abort(reason)) }
    }
  }, cont)
}

// The four effects no policy field decides: recomputation over values the program
// already holds. The guard performs them unchanged.
let with_decode_json = (cont) -> {
  handle DecodeJSON((value, resume) -> {
    let reply = perform DecodeJSON(value)
    resume(reply)
  }, cont)
}

let with_eyg_parse = (cont) -> {
  handle EYGParse((value, resume) -> {
    let reply = perform EYGParse(value)
    resume(reply)
  }, cont)
}

let with_hash = (cont) -> {
  handle Hash((value, resume) -> {
    let reply = perform Hash(value)
    resume(reply)
  }, cont)
}

let with_sign = (cont) -> {
  handle Sign((value, resume) -> {
    let reply = perform Sign(value)
    resume(reply)
  }, cont)
}

// The guarded run: every label named, the effect-performing handlers wrapped
// around the body in the order that keeps a handler's own re-perform inside the
// rule that decides it - `read_directory` outside `search` - and the model's code
// last, in the body the entry hands in.
let build = (policy, ctx, body) -> {
  with_read_directory(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
    with_read_file(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
      with_search(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
        with_write_file(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
          with_append_file(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
            with_delete_file(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
              with_make_directory(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
                with_cwd(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
                  with_env(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
                    with_fetch(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
                      with_standard_in(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
                        with_create_key(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
                          with_now(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
                            with_random(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
                              with_flip(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
                                with_sleep(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
                                  with_standard_out(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
                                    with_standard_error(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
                                      with_exit(policy, ctx, (_) -> {
                                        with_decode_json((_) -> {
                                          with_eyg_parse((_) -> {
                                            with_hash((_) -> {
                                              with_sign((k) -> {
                                        let _ = body(k)
                                        []
                                      })
                                            })
                                          })
                                        })
                                      })
                                    })
                                  })
                                })
                              })
                            })
                          })
                        })
                      })
                    })
                  })
                })
              })
            })
          })
        })
      })
    })
  })
}

{build}
